00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.760
You know, there's not just one AI, there are millions or billions of them.

29
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:17.240
Even if we're not dead, even if we continue to maybe live for a couple of years or even decades, we no longer have any control over the future.

30
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.640
And there's no way for us to get it back.

31
00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.680
Okay, you've already slipped in a base assumption there.

32
00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:30.040
Um so I before we get to that base assumption, because it is certainly on the list of where this sort of breaks bad, a very clear definition.

33
00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.960
So autonomous AI that can outperform humans at everything, that's the one that we're worried about.

34
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:38.520
Uh, we need to understand the mile marker, which is what's the point of no return.

35
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:45.000
You've talked about this before, so I think we can hit this one quick, which is we're close, you don't quite know.

36
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:51.160
The key distinction, a human using AI is not the same as a human controlling it.

37
00:02:51.160 --> 00:03:02.760
On the Twimble AI podcast, Stanford Professor Chris Potts explains how language models are becoming experimental tools for linguists while disrupting traditional NLP research.

38
00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.880
